<section class="story-section" id="problem"><p class="eyebrow">01 / The problem before</p><h2>You rarely get the<br />question right first time.</h2><p>Imagine asking for a travel plan. Then you change the destination. Then you ask, “Can we make it shorter?” Each request depends on what came before. A useful conversation needs more than a good answer to an isolated question.</p><p>Instruction-following training had already made progress. The next step in this story puts that ability into a back-and-forth exchange.</p></section>
<section class="story-section" id="idea"><p class="eyebrow">02 / The big idea</p><h2>The follow-up<br />becomes the interface.</h2><p>On November 30, 2022, OpenAI introduced ChatGPT as a research preview. It was designed for dialogue: answering follow-up questions, responding to corrections, and sometimes challenging a mistaken premise.</p><p>Human trainers wrote conversations, playing both user and assistant. They also compared alternative replies. <strong>Reinforcement learning from human feedback</strong>, or RLHF, uses rewards learned from those comparisons to guide training. ChatGPT adapted the approach used for InstructGPT to conversation.</p><p><strong>Context</strong> is the information supplied to a model for its next response. Earlier messages can make “it” or “that city” meaningful. This is different from changing the model’s <strong>parameters</strong>—the numbers learned during training. The toy makes that distinction visible.</p>
<figure class="idea-diagram conversation-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 400 400" role="img" aria-labelledby="chat-diagram-title chat-diagram-desc"><title id="chat-diagram-title">A follow-up carries a conversation with it</title><desc id="chat-diagram-desc">Earlier messages and a new question form the context for the next reply. The model uses that input without a training step.</desc><g text-anchor="middle" fill="currentColor"><rect x="10" y="10" width="380" height="70" rx="5" fill="var(--paper)" stroke="var(--line)"/><text x="200" y="40" font-size="22">Earlier messages</text><text x="200" y="65" font-size="18">“Plan a trip to Kyoto.”</text><text x="200" y="115" font-size="30">+</text><rect x="10" y="135" width="380" height="70" rx="5" fill="var(--accent)"/><text x="200" y="165" font-size="22">New question</text><text x="200" y="190" font-size="18">“Which city are we visiting?”</text><text x="200" y="241" font-size="30">↓</text><rect x="40" y="260" width="320" height="60" rx="5" fill="var(--ink)"/><text x="200" y="298" font-size="25" fill="var(--surface)">Model uses this context</text><text x="200" y="357" font-size="30">↓</text><text x="200" y="393" font-size="25">The next reply</text></g></svg><figcaption>A simplified conversation loop. Context supplies information; training changes the model itself.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="story-section toy-section" id="play"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Play with it</p><h2>Keep the conversation.<br />Or lose the thread.</h2><Discovery slug="chatgpt" /><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to change the history. Without the destination message, this scripted reader asks which city you mean.</p></noscript><ConversationToy client:load /></section>
<section class="story-section" id="mattered"><p class="eyebrow">04 / Why it mattered</p><h2>A reply can become<br />your next starting point.</h2><p>Think of the interface as a draft you can answer back to. You can refine a request over several turns instead of packing everything into one perfect prompt. That is the interaction this chapter invites you to explore.</p><p>The launch also documented confident errors and sensitivity to wording. A conversational answer can feel natural while still being wrong. Next, the input itself expands: what if the conversation includes a picture?</p><a class="next-chapter" href="/chapters/gpt-4/"><span>Next · 2023</span><strong>Beyond text: GPT-4 ↗</strong><span>What changes when a model can read a picture too?</span></a></section>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="deeper"><summary>Go deeper <span>Dialogue training and context</span></summary><div class="disclosure-body"><h3>Training for both sides of a conversation</h3><p>The launch describes fine-tuning a GPT-3.5-series model using human-written dialogue, mixed with InstructGPT examples converted into conversations. Trainers ranked alternative assistant messages; learned reward models then guided Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO), a reinforcement-learning update algorithm. This is training before use, rather than a new training run for each chat turn.</p><h3>Condition on the conversation</h3><p>A simplified next-reply model is <code>P(y | H, u) = ∏t P(yt | H, u, y&lt;t)</code>. H is the included history, u the new user message, and y the reply, built from tokens—pieces of text. A changed history changes the input to the same learned model. This notation is a teaching abstraction, not a specification of ChatGPT’s internal message format.</p><p>A context window is the amount of input and output a model can work with at once. Our toy discards whole messages to illustrate missing context; it does not model a particular product’s truncation, summaries or saved memory. The rule uses the latest explicit destination in a retained user message. It cannot infer destinations from arbitrary language.</p><h3>What the launch established</h3><p>The announcement demonstrated dialogue with selected examples and opened a public research preview. It did not provide a general dialogue benchmark score or enough architecture details to reconstruct the system. Its stated limitations include plausible false answers, inconsistent responses to rephrasing, verbosity and guessing user intent when clarification would help.</p></div></details>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="sources"><summary>Sources <span>Official announcement and training paper</span></summary><div class="disclosure-body"><ol class="source-list"><li><a href={paperLink("chatgpt-launch")}>OpenAI · Introducing ChatGPT · November 30, 2022</a><p>Introduction: research preview and conversational capabilities. Methods: GPT-3.5, dialogue demonstrations, converted InstructGPT data, ranked replies and PPO. Limitations: false answers, prompt sensitivity, verbosity and clarification failures. Samples are demonstrations rather than aggregate benchmark results.</p></li><li><a href={sourceLink("ouyang-2022-neurips-full-text")}>Ouyang et al. · Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback · 2022</a><p>Introduction and Figure 2: the instruction-following predecessor, human demonstrations, reward learning and policy training. Section 3.4: language-model policies and reinforcement learning.</p></li><li><a href={paperLink("gpt-4-report")}>OpenAI · GPT-4 Technical Report · 2023</a><p>Introduction and Section 2: next-token prediction and limited context; the next chapter’s combination of image and text inputs with text outputs.</p></li></ol><p>The travel dialogue, diagram and rule-based reader are invented teaching examples. The discussion of iterative drafting is an interpretation of the conversational interaction, not a measured result.</p></div></details>
